<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch09_P03_The_similar_step</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>(a) In the proof above, \(f(1 + 2p)\) is a multiple of \(p\) ‘in the same way’ as \(f(1 + p)\). Find the other factor, in terms of \(a\), \(b\) and \(p\).</p>
<div>\(f(1 + 2p) = p \times\) ( [[input:ans1]] ) [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) The proof needs \(f(n)\) to be a multiple of \(p\) at three values of \(n\), not just two. To see why, give an example of a quadratic \(f(n)\), with whole-number coefficients, for which \(f(1) = {@pp@}\) and \(f({@q1@}) = {@pp@}\).</p>
<div>\(f(n) =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Expand, and use \(a + b + c = p\) to replace the terms left over:</p>
\[f(1 + 2p) = a(1 + 4p + 4p^2) + b(1 + 2p) + c = 4ap^2 + 4ap + 2bp + (a + b + c) = 4ap^2 + 4ap + 2bp + p = p(4ap + 4a + 2b + 1).\]
<p>(b) \(f(1) = f({@q1@}) = {@pp@}\) means that \(f(n) - {@pp@}\) has the roots 1 and {@q1@}, so \(f(n) = k(n - 1)(n - {@q1@}) + {@pp@}\) for any whole number \(k \neq 0\); for \(k = 1\), \(f(n) = {@ta2@}\). So a quadratic can equal {@pp@} at two values of \(n\). It cannot at three, because \(f(n) - {@pp@} = 0\) is a quadratic equation, with at most two solutions. For \(k = 1\), \(f({@q2@}) = {@val3t@} = {@pp@} \times {@2*pp+1@}\), which is not prime: exactly what the proof predicts.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
